{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001's argument that safety is vacuous relies entirely on properties of the *replay* path — no concurrency, no interleaving, no observable intermediate states during reconstruction. But the problem asks what the log must require 'of its writers to survive at-least-once delivery,' which concerns the live delivery path where writers retry, not the replay path. A writer retrying an append in a live system that is concurrently serving reads or processing other writes does face observable intermediate states. SRC_001's mechanism conflates replay-time guarantees (which the log's total order provides) with delivery-time requirements (which writers must satisfy). The total order of the log ensures deterministic replay, but it does not eliminate concurrency during the live write/retry path. SRC_001's own countercondition #2 concedes that external side effects during retry make idempotency insufficient, yet the main claim never reconciles this with the problem's explicit focus on what *writers* must guarantee.","target":"9699d675246fec6f9bf2f683a470966ef8edba6e2221adada2e16826b5060fbd"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002's central distinguishing example — 'an idempotent operation that temporarily deletes and re-inserts a log entry during its second execution' — is architecturally impossible in an append-only event log, which by definition prohibits deletion. The mechanism SRC_002 uses to establish that safety is strictly stronger than idempotency depends on an operation (delete + re-insert) that cannot occur in the specific system the problem asks about. This undercuts the claim that safety is the property the append-only log must require, because the scenario that makes safety non-trivial is excluded by the system's own constraints. Moreover, SRC_002's own countercondition #1 concedes that in a single-threaded system with no concurrent observers — exactly what deterministic total-order replay provides — safety's extra guarantee is vacuous and 'pure idempotency suffices,' directly contradicting the main claim.","target":"f5f08329e62a9d4b1b070bb21b476b55840e8a0fec40b8dd74366ea0ebfe7a5a"}]}